#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148527} :activity/id #uuid "6cfee12a-1cb0-5520-abc8-68505a88af79" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049657 :topic/id #uuid "08c0f60c-daa6-535e-8559-0b890fd64487" :topic/math-academy-id 2472} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148687 :tutorial/content "To model an exponential decay situation, it is often useful to start by setting up a function which represents the exponential decay. Then, we can evaluate that function as needed.\n\nFor example, suppose that a car's value depreciates by one-fifth every year. If the car was purchased for $\\[math]25\\,000$, then what function gives the car's value after $t$ years?\n\nFirst, note that if the car's value reduces by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 5\n$$\n\nevery year, then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 5\n$$\n\nof its value remains every year.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 25\\,000$ is the **initial value** of the car (in dollars).\n- $b = \\dfrac 4 5$ is the **decay factor**.\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the car's value after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 25\\,000\\left(\\dfrac 4 5\\right)^t\n$$\n\nTo find the value of the car after $5$ years, we substitute $t=5$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(5) = 25000 \\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})^{5} = 8192\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth $\\[/math] 8\\,192$ in $5$ years." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc222367-f615-54c3-85ae-9459900ad1cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1968 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ea762fc0-a1cb-53ed-ba8b-e09b7fb84745" :step/math-academy-id 7114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147570 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147207 :question/id #uuid "34889441-479d-59e6-b9c6-a3972ff9ea60" :question/math-academy-id "e-2068"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64181d45-ac95-5c6c-86bb-407a0a3cb811" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050625}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147938}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Decay Using a Decay Rate"} :step/id #uuid "901a35f0-83a6-5c46-ac56-6eb267f4959f" :step/math-academy-id 7115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147571 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147208 :question/id #uuid "06d40324-f901-5e87-93c1-318a819eb635" :question/math-academy-id "e-2070"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "873b588f-41f6-561a-99bf-f6764a9338ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050339}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147954}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Decay Using a Decay Factor"} :step/id #uuid "a23a1883-2c0b-531a-a8b0-5f58e4ea4a0a" :step/math-academy-id 7117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148688 :tutorial/content "It's common to use a **decay rate** (rather than a decay factor) when describing exponential decay. A decay rate is typically expressed using a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that after treatment with a particular antibiotic, the number of infected cells in a specimen decreased by $30\\%$ per hour. If there were approximately $5$ million infected cells at the beginning of the treatment, how many infected cells remained after $3$ hours?\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the number of infected cells after $t$ hours. The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1-r)^t\n$$\n\nNotice that this is very similar to the formula for exponential growth. The difference here is that we have a minus $(-)$ instead of a plus $(+)$ in the parentheses.\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of infected cells (in millions).\n- $r = 0.30$ is the decay rate ($30\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny = 5(1-0.3)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above gives\n\n$$\ny(t) =5(0.7)^{t}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function for $t=3$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(3) = 5(0.7)^{3} = 1.715\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ hours, there were $1.715$ million infected cells.\n\nWhen writing down a formula for exponential decay, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If we're given a **decay rate** (usually expressed as a percent), then we use the formula $y(t) = a(1-r)^t$.\n- If we're given a **decay factor**, then we use the formula $y(t) = ab^t$." :tutorial/id #uuid "eac27d5f-01b7-53c0-a804-7d42daa3f1e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1969 :tutorial/title "Modeling Exponential Decay Using a Decay Rate"} :step/id #uuid "21e9116a-98fc-52ac-a3d4-5c0ce2837688" :step/math-academy-id 7123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148528}}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Decay With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147672}]}
